            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`length` traverses the whole sequence. Comparing its result against 0 asks an O(n) question whose answer the first element already gives, and never terminates on a circular list."
              },
              "id": "length-emptiness-test",
              "properties": {
                "category": "performance",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`length` traverses the whole sequence. Comparing its result against 0 asks an O(n) question whose answer the first element already gives, and never terminates on a circular list.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a (length …) call used only to decide whether a sequence is empty, which walks the whole sequence"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`append` and `nconc` copy or walk their first argument. Passing the accumulator as that argument on every round re-walks everything gathered so far, so building n elements costs O(n²). Pushing onto the front and reversing once at the end costs O(n)."
              },
              "id": "quadratic-accumulation",
              "properties": {
                "category": "performance",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`append` and `nconc` copy or walk their first argument. Passing the accumulator as that argument on every round re-walks everything gathered so far, so building n elements costs O(n²). Pushing onto the front and reversing once at the end costs O(n).",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a loop that grows a sequence by appending to its own accumulated value, which is quadratic"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`member`, `assoc`, `find`, and `position` walk their sequence. Calling one inside a loop on a collection the loop does not change multiplies the two lengths, where hoisting the collection into a hash table before the loop makes each lookup constant."
              },
              "id": "linear-search-in-loop",
              "properties": {
                "category": "performance",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`member`, `assoc`, `find`, and `position` walk their sequence. Calling one inside a loop on a collection the loop does not change multiplies the two lengths, where hoisting the collection into a hash table before the loop makes each lookup constant.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a loop that linearly searches the same collection on every round, which is quadratic"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `copy-*` call exists to keep a caller's sequence safe from mutation. When the value goes straight into an operator specified never to modify its argument, nothing is being protected and the copy is allocated only to be discarded."
              },
              "id": "unnecessary-copy",
              "properties": {
                "category": "allocation",
                "fixable": true,
                "rationale": "A `copy-*` call exists to keep a caller's sequence safe from mutation. When the value goes straight into an operator specified never to modify its argument, nothing is being protected and the copy is allocated only to be discarded.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a copy-seq/copy-list result passed to an operator that only reads it, so the copy is garbage"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Copying a sequence so that a destructive operator may safely destroy the copy is exactly what the operator's non-destructive twin does, in one pass instead of two."
              },
              "id": "copy-before-destructive",
              "properties": {
                "category": "allocation",
                "fixable": true,
                "rationale": "Copying a sequence so that a destructive operator may safely destroy the copy is exactly what the operator's non-destructive twin does, in one pass instead of two.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": [
                  "destructive"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a fresh copy fed to a destructive operator, where one non-destructive operator does both"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A constructor whose arguments do not depend on the loop produces an equivalent object every round. Building it once before the loop replaces n allocations with one."
              },
              "id": "loop-invariant-allocation",
              "properties": {
                "category": "allocation",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A constructor whose arguments do not depend on the loop produces an equivalent object every round. Building it once before the loop replaces n allocations with one.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": [
                  "pedantic"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an allocation inside a loop whose arguments never mention the loop, so every round builds the same object"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A symbol qualified with an implementation's package does not exist in any other implementation, so a file naming one fails to read there rather than failing to work. Guarding it with a reader conditional is what makes the dependency explicit and the file portable."
              },
              "id": "implementation-package-symbol",
              "properties": {
                "category": "portability",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A symbol qualified with an implementation's package does not exist in any other implementation, so a file naming one fails to read there rather than failing to work. Guarding it with a reader conditional is what makes the dependency explicit and the file portable.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a symbol from an implementation's own package, outside any reader conditional guarding it"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A namestring is parsed by the host's pathname syntax, so a backslash separator, a drive letter, or an absolute root means something on one platform and something else — or nothing — on another. `make-pathname` and `merge-pathnames` name a file without naming a filesystem."
              },
              "id": "unportable-pathname",
              "properties": {
                "category": "portability",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A namestring is parsed by the host's pathname syntax, so a backslash separator, a drive letter, or an absolute root means something on one platform and something else — or nothing — on another. `make-pathname` and `merge-pathnames` name a file without naming a filesystem.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a filesystem call given a string that spells one host's path syntax"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The standard does not fix the mapping from character codes to characters, so a numeric literal names a different character on an implementation that does not use ASCII — and names nothing recognisable to a reader on any implementation."
              },
              "id": "ascii-code-char",
              "properties": {
                "category": "portability",
                "fixable": true,
                "rationale": "The standard does not fix the mapping from character codes to characters, so a numeric literal names a different character on an implementation that does not use ASCII — and names nothing recognisable to a reader on any implementation.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a (code-char N) call with a literal code, which assumes ASCII and hides which character it means"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Binary floating point cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly, so a value that should equal 0.1 usually differs from the literal `0.1` in the last bits. The comparison then succeeds or fails according to the rounding of every operation that produced it."
              },
              "id": "float-equality",
              "properties": {
                "category": "numeric-precision",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Binary floating point cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly, so a value that should equal 0.1 usually differs from the literal `0.1` in the last bits. The comparison then succeeds or fails according to the rounding of every operation that produced it.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an exact equality test against a float literal, whose result depends on rounding upstream"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A special variable is visible to every thread and every caller, so assigning to one from inside a function makes the function's result depend on call history and its behaviour under concurrency undefined without external synchronisation."
              },
              "id": "global-mutation-in-function",
              "properties": {
                "category": "concurrency",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A special variable is visible to every thread and every caller, so assigning to one from inside a function makes the function's result depend on call history and its behaviour under concurrency undefined without external synchronisation.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a function body assigning to a *special-looking* global, which is shared mutable state"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Reading a shared place, deciding on the result, and then writing it is three operations. A second thread that runs between the read and the write sees the same 'not present' answer, and one of the two writes is silently lost."
              },
              "id": "check-then-act",
              "properties": {
                "category": "concurrency",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Reading a shared place, deciding on the result, and then writing it is three operations. A second thread that runs between the read and the write sees the same 'not present' answer, and one of the two writes is silently lost.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a conditional that tests a place and then assigns to the same place, which another thread can interleave"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "These operators turn data into code or into symbols. When the data is computed, nothing in the source says what will run or what will be interned, and any path from outside the program to that argument is a path to arbitrary execution or to unbounded symbol-table growth."
              },
              "id": "eval-of-non-constant",
              "properties": {
                "category": "security",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "These operators turn data into code or into symbols. When the data is computed, nothing in the source says what will run or what will be interned, and any path from outside the program to that argument is a path to arbitrary execution or to unbounded symbol-table growth.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an eval, intern, or read-from-string whose argument is not a literal the source shows"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`*read-eval*` is true by default, so `read` executes any `#.(…)` in its input as part of reading it. Data from outside the program therefore becomes code before the program has inspected one form of it."
              },
              "id": "read-without-read-eval-guard",
              "properties": {
                "category": "security",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`*read-eval*` is true by default, so `read` executes any `#.(…)` in its input as part of reading it. Data from outside the program therefore becomes code before the program has inspected one form of it.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a read of external data with no enclosing *read-eval* nil binding, so #. in the input executes"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A command line assembled from strings is parsed by a shell, so any interpolated value that contains a shell metacharacter becomes syntax rather than data. Passing the program and its arguments as separate list elements removes the shell from the path."
              },
              "id": "subprocess-string-building",
              "properties": {
                "category": "security",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A command line assembled from strings is parsed by a shell, so any interpolated value that contains a shell metacharacter becomes syntax rather than data. Passing the program and its arguments as separate list elements removes the shell from the path.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a subprocess launched with a command line built by format or concatenate, which a value can escape"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `close` in the straight-line body runs only when the body completes normally. Any condition signalled, `return-from`, or `throw` between the `open` and the `close` skips it and leaks the file handle. `with-open-file` closes on every exit path."
              },
              "id": "unclosed-stream",
              "properties": {
                "category": "resource",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A `close` in the straight-line body runs only when the body completes normally. Any condition signalled, `return-from`, or `throw` between the `open` and the `close` skips it and leaks the file handle. `with-open-file` closes on every exit path.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a let-bound (open …) stream with no unwind-protect, so a non-local exit leaks the handle"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`with-open-file` closes its stream as the body exits, so the value the body returns is a closed stream. Every operation the caller performs on it fails."
              },
              "id": "stream-escapes-with-open-file",
              "properties": {
                "category": "resource",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`with-open-file` closes its stream as the body exits, so the value the body returns is a closed stream. Every operation the caller performs on it fails.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a with-open-file whose last body form is the stream, which the form has already closed"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A handler on `error` catches every failure the body can signal, including ones written after the handler. A body that returns a constant leaves the caller unable to tell failure from a legitimate result, and leaves no record that anything went wrong."
              },
              "id": "handler-case-swallows-error",
              "properties": {
                "category": "conditions",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A handler on `error` catches every failure the body can signal, including ones written after the handler. A body that returns a constant leaves the caller unable to tell failure from a legitimate result, and leaves no record that anything went wrong.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a handler-case clause on a broad condition type whose body discards the condition entirely"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`handler-case` selects the first clause whose type the condition is of. A clause on a supertype therefore shadows every later clause on one of its subtypes, and the narrower handler — which is the one somebody thought about — never runs."
              },
              "id": "unreachable-handler-clause",
              "properties": {
                "category": "conditions",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`handler-case` selects the first clause whose type the condition is of. A clause on a supertype therefore shadows every later clause on one of its subtypes, and the narrower handler — which is the one somebody thought about — never runs.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a handler-case clause whose condition type an earlier clause already covers, so it never runs"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Nothing in the syntax of a *use* site distinguishes a special variable from a lexical one, or a constant from either. The earmuff conventions are what carry that, and they carry it only while they are applied without exception."
              },
              "id": "definition-naming",
              "properties": {
                "category": "naming",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Nothing in the syntax of a *use* site distinguishes a special variable from a lexical one, or a constant from either. The earmuff conventions are what carry that, and they carry it only while they are applied without exception.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": [
                  "pedantic",
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a defconstant/defvar/defparameter whose name lacks the +constant+ or *special* marker"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A caller decides whether to copy an argument from the callee's name. A function that applies a destructive operator to a parameter, under a name with no `n` prefix and no `!` suffix, tells the caller the opposite of the truth."
              },
              "id": "destructive-function-naming",
              "properties": {
                "category": "naming",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A caller decides whether to copy an argument from the callee's name. A function that applies a destructive operator to a parameter, under a name with no `n` prefix and no `!` suffix, tells the caller the opposite of the truth.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": [
                  "pedantic"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a function that destructively modifies one of its own parameters but is not named for it"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Common Lisp docstring lives in the image, not only in the file: `(documentation 'f 'function)` and `describe` read it at runtime. A definition without one cannot be explained from the REPL."
              },
              "id": "missing-docstring",
              "properties": {
                "category": "documentation",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A Common Lisp docstring lives in the image, not only in the file: `(documentation 'f 'function)` and `describe` read it at runtime. A definition without one cannot be explained from the REPL.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": [
                  "pedantic"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a definition with no docstring, so `documentation` and `describe` have nothing to report"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`(declare (ignore x))` states that the body will not refer to `x`. A body that refers to it anyway makes the two statements contradict, which most implementations diagnose; a declaration naming something the lambda list does not bind silences nothing and hides the parameter that really is unused."
              },
              "id": "ignore-declaration-conflict",
              "properties": {
                "category": "declaration",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`(declare (ignore x))` states that the body will not refer to `x`. A body that refers to it anyway makes the two statements contradict, which most implementations diagnose; a declaration naming something the lambda list does not bind silences nothing and hides the parameter that really is unused.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an (ignore x) declaration whose variable the body uses, or which names no parameter at all"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The standard does not say which of two values for the same optimization quality a compiler takes, so a declaration that gives both means something different on every implementation — and usually something different from what its author intended."
              },
              "id": "contradictory-optimize",
              "properties": {
                "category": "declaration",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "The standard does not say which of two values for the same optimization quality a compiler takes, so a declaration that gives both means something different on every implementation — and usually something different from what its author intended.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an optimize declaration naming one quality twice with different values"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The standard slot options are a closed set. An option outside it is either ignored — so the slot silently lacks the initform, reader, or type it appears to declare — or signalled, depending on the implementation."
              },
              "id": "defclass-slot-option",
              "properties": {
                "category": "object-system",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "The standard slot options are a closed set. An option outside it is either ignored — so the slot silently lacks the initform, reader, or type it appears to declare — or signalled, depending on the implementation.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a defclass slot option that is not one of the standard ones, or an :initarg that is not a keyword"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A method's lambda list must be congruent with its generic function's: same number of required parameters, same number of optionals, and `&rest`/`&key` present in both or neither. `defmethod` signals when it is not, so the file fails to load."
              },
              "id": "method-lambda-list-mismatch",
              "properties": {
                "category": "object-system",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A method's lambda list must be congruent with its generic function's: same number of required parameters, same number of optionals, and `&rest`/`&key` present in both or neither. `defmethod` signals when it is not, so the file fails to load.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a defmethod whose required-parameter count differs from its defgeneric in the same file"
              }
            },
